     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Bleed b/n the arachnoid and pia mater; head trauma = the MCC overall (traumatic SAH = USU considered a separate dx); spont SAH USU results from ruptured aneurysms, w a "
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       },
       {
        "type": "highlight",
        "attrs": {
         "hex": "FF0000"
        }
       }
      ],
      "text": "congenital saccular (berry) aneurysm the cause in ≈ 85%"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Aneurysmal hemorrhage may occur @ any age but = MC from age 40 to 65; less common Eti are mycotic aneurysms, arteriovenous malformations, amyloid deposition and bleed disorders"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Blood → a chem meningitis that commonly raises ICP for days or wks; 2/2 vasospasm may cause focal ischemia (≈ 25% develop TIA or ischemic CVA signs), w vasospasm and infarct risk highest b/n 72 hrs and 10 days; 2/2 acute hydrocephalus = common; rebleeding sometimes occurs, most often w.in 7 days"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 0,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": 0,
     "spaceBefore": 6,
